on is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nKewei Xu\n\nKewei Xu\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nHaoming Xu\n\nHaoming Xu\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nHaiwen Hong\n\nHaiwen Hong\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nShow all 11 authors Hide\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nRequest full-text PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research, you can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\n## No full-text available\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCharacterizing Vision-Language-Action Models across XPUs: Constraints and Acceleration for On-Robot Deployment — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/characterizing-vision-language-action-models-across-xpus-constraints-and/wrk_d11ee0e7326ef922b10ab8432e164359/version/art_b0ba3633f9768d4c4e48d22f02437233)\nciteturn32107search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Characterizing Vision-Language-Action Models across XPUs: Constraints and Acceleration for On-Robot Deploymenticml · 2026Presentation Characterizing Vision-Language-Action Models across XPUs: Constraints and Acceleration for On-Robot DeploymentICML 2026 regular · 2026 Characterizing Vision-Language-Action Models across XPUs: Constraints and Acceleration for On-Robot DeploymentarXiv · 2026 ... We present a systematic framework for low-cost VLA deployment via model--hardware co-characterization. ... Second, using fine-grained SM tracing and Roofline analysis, we uncover a consistent two-phase inference pattern: a compute-bound VLM backbone followed by a memory-bound Action Expert, which induces phase-dependent underutilization and hardware inefficiency.\n\n# Characterizing Vision-Language-Action Models across XPUs: Constraints and Acceleration for On-Robot Deployment\n\nKaijun Zhou, Qiwei Chen, Da Peng, Zhiyang Li, Xijun Li, Jinyu Gu\n\nIcml 2026 Regular 2026 · OpenReview\n\n## Versions\n\nCharacterizing Vision-Language-Action Models across XPUs: Constraints and Acceleration for On-Robot Deploymenticml · 2026PresentationCharacterizing Vision-Language-Action Models across XPUs: Constraints and Acceleration for On-Robot DeploymentICML 2026 regular · 2026Characterizing Vision-Language-Action Models across XPUs: Constraints and Acceleration for On-Robot DeploymentarXiv · 2026\n\nRead Paper Want to read\n\n[Button: Want to read][Button: Reading][Button: Read]\n\n## Abstract\n\nVision-Language-Action (VLA) models are promising for generalist robot control, but on-robot deployment is bottlenecked by real-time inference under tight cost and energy budgets. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHui Xue's research works | Alibaba Group, Hangzhou and other places (https://www.researchgate.net/scientific-contributions/Hui-Xue-2152817227/publications/3)\nciteturn32107search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; January 2026 ... Overall, these results show that safety in frontier models is inherently multidimensional--shaped by modality, language, and evaluation scheme, underscoring the need for standardized safety evaluations to accurately assess real-world risk and guide responsible model development and deployment. ... How Controllable Are Large Language Models?\nAggregating our evaluations into safety leaderboards and model safety profiles across multiple evaluation modes reveals a sharply heterogeneous safety landscape. While GPT-5.2 demonstrates consistently strong and balanced safety performance across evaluations, other models exhibit pronounced trade-offs among benchmark safety, adversarial alignment, multilingual generalization, and regulatory compliance. Both language and vision-language modalities show significant vulnerability under adversarial evaluation, with all models degrading substantially despite strong results on standard benchmarks. Text-to-image models achieve relatively stronger alignment in regulated visual risk categories, yet remain brittle under adversarial or semantically ambiguous prompts. Overall, these results show that safety in frontier models is inherently multidimensional--shaped by modality, language, and evaluation scheme, underscoring the need for standardized safety evaluations to accurately assess real-world risk and guide responsible model development and deployment.\n\n[Button: Read more]\n\nDownload\n\nHow Controllable Are Large Language Models? A Unified Evaluation across Behavioral Granularities\n\nConference Paper\n\nJanuary 2026\n\n·\n\n3 Reads\n\n·\n\n1 Citation\n\nZiwen Xu\n\n·\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVLA-Arena: An Open-Source Framework for Benchmarking Vision-Language-Action Models (https://proceedings.mlr.press/v306/zhang26cp.html)\nciteturn32107search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: yesterday; To foster research addressing these model limitations and ensure reproducibility, we provide the complete VLA-Arena framework, including an end-to-end toolchain from task definition to automated evaluation and the VLA-Arena-S/M/L datasets for fine-tuning. ... `%0 Conference Paper %T VLA-Arena: An Open-Source Framework for Benchmarking Vision-Language-Action Models %A Borong Zhang %A Jiahao Li %A Jiachen Shen %A Yuhao Zhang %A Yishuai Cai %A Yuanpei Chen %A Juntao Dai %A Jiaming Ji %A Yaodong Yang %B Proceedings of the 43rd International Conference on Machine Learning %C Proceedings of Machine Learning Research %D 2026 %E Tong Zhang %E Miroslav Dudik %E Martin Jaggi %E Alekh Agarwal %E Sharon Li %E Dale Schuurmans %E Jerry Zhu %E Felix Berkenkamp %E Hanze Dong %E Alberto Bietti %F pmlr-v306-zhang26cp %I PMLR %P 156567--156614 %U https://proceedings.mlr.press/v306/zhang26cp.html %V 306 %X While Vision-Language-Action models (VLAs) are rapidly advancing toward generalist robot policies, quantitatively characterizing their capability boundaries and failure modes remains challenging.\n\n# VLA-Arena: An Open-Source Framework for Benchmarking Vision-Language-Action Models\n\nBorong Zhang, Jiahao Li, Jiachen Shen, Yuhao Zhang, Yishuai Cai, Yuanpei Chen, Juntao Dai, Jiaming Ji, Yaodong Yang\n\nProceedings of the 43rd International Conference on Machine Learning, PMLR 306:156567-156614, 2026.\n\n#### Abstract\n\nWhile Vision-Language-Action models (VLAs) are rapidly advancing toward generalist robot policies, quantitatively characterizing their capability boundaries and failure modes remains challenging. To address this, we introduce VLA-Arena, a comprehensive benchmark. It features a novel structured task design framework to quantify difficulty across three orthogonal axes: (1) Task Structure, (2) Language Command, and (3) Visual Observation. This allows us to systematically design tasks with fine-grained difficulty levels, enabling a precise measurement of model capability frontiers. For task structure, VLA-Arena comprises 11 task suites organized into four dimensions: Safety, Distractor, Extrapolation, and Long Horizon, totaling 170 tasks. Additionally, model rank reversals across L0-L2 validate that each level provides non-redundant insights. To foster research addressing these model limitations and ensure reproducibility, we provide the complete VLA-Arena framework, including an end-to-end toolchain from task definition to automated evaluation and the VLA-Arena-S/M/L datasets for fine-tuning. Our benchmark, datasets, models, and leaderboard are publicly available at https://vla-arena.github.io.} } `\n\nEndnote\n\n`%0 Conference Paper %T VLA-Arena: An Open-Source Framework for Benchmarking Vision-Language-Action Models %A Borong Zhang %A Jiahao Li %A Jiachen Shen %A Yuhao Zhang %A Yishuai Cai %A Yuanpei Chen %A Juntao Dai %A Jiaming Ji %A Yaodong Yang %B Proceedings of the 43rd International Conference on Machine Learning %C Proceedings of Machine Learning Research %D 2026 %E Tong Zhang %E Miroslav Dudik %E Martin Jaggi %E Alekh Agarwal %E Sharon Li %E Dale Schuurmans %E Jerry Zhu %E Felix Berkenkamp %E Hanze Dong %E Alberto Bietti %F pmlr-v306-zhang26cp %I PMLR %P 156567--156614 %U https://proceedings.mlr.press/v306/zhang26cp.html %V 306 %X While Vision-Language-Action models (VLAs) are rapidly advancing toward generalist robot policies, quantitatively characterizing their capability boundaries and failure modes remains challenging. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Ningyu Zhang 0001 (https://dblp.dagstuhl.de/pid/139/4181-1.html)\nciteturn32107search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; How Controllable Are Large Language Models? ... ACL (1) 2026: 31269-31299 ... Why Steering Works: Toward a Unified View of Language Model Parameter Dynamics.\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/conf/acl/XuXXHHXZSZCD26\n\nZiwen Xu, Kewei Xu, Haoming Xu, Haiwen Hong, Longtao Huang, Hui Xue, Ningyu Zhang, Yongliang Shen, Guozhou Zheng, Huajun Chen, Shumin Deng:\nHow Controllable Are Large Language Models? A Unified Evaluation across Behavioral Granularities. ACL (1) 2026: 31269-31299\n  * Image: Conference and Workshop Papers\n\n[c140]\n\n    * view\n\n      * electronic edition via DOI (open access)\n      * electronic edition @ aclanthology.org (open access)\n      * details & citations\n\nauthority control:\n\n      *  \n\n    * export record\n\n      * BibTeX\n      * RIS\n      * RDF N-Triples\n      * RDF Turtle\n      * RDF/XML\n      * XML\n      * plain text\n\ndblp key:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVLAモデルの特性化:エッジAIアーキテクチャのための行動生成ボトルネックの同定〖JST機械翻訳〗 | 文献情報 | J-GLOBAL 科学技術総合リンクセンター (https://jglobal.jst.go.jp/detail?JGLOBAL_ID=202602216622110977)\nciteturn32107search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last month; Characterizing VLA Models: Identifying the Action Generation Bottleneck for Edge AI Archite