a stark architectural asymmetry: the autoregressive action-generation phase consumes approximately 75% of total end-to-end latency despite representing only a fraction of model parameters. This finding inverts conventional assumptions about Vision-Language-Action model deployment. Vision encoding, which processes raw pixels through SigLIP and DINOv2 backbones into feature embeddings, runs efficiently on edge accelerators. The reasoning engine—a decoder-only Transformer performing cross-modal reasoning—also completes within reasonable time bounds. Yet the final stage, where the model translates internal representations into motor commands through either discrete action tokenization or continuous trajectory generation, emerges as a severe memory-bandwidth bottleneck that existing approaches to robot deployment have largely overlooked.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBeyond LLM Serving: Characterizing Vision-Language-Action Workloads for Embodied AI System Design (https://www.catalyzex.com/paper/beyond-llm-serving-characterizing-vision)\nciteturn32107search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 days ago; Crawled: yesterday; # Title:Beyond LLM Serving: Characterizing Vision-Language-Action Workloads for Embodied AI System Design ... Oct 04, 2026 ... We characterize four representative VLA models on an edge GPU server and two onboard SoCs, using single-inference profiling and 43,200 closed-loop episodes. ... These results guide joint design of VLA model architectures, hardware, and runtime policies.\n\n# Title:Beyond LLM Serving: Characterizing Vision-Language-Action Workloads for Embodied AI System Design\n\n### Paper and Code\n\nOct 04, 2026\n\nAuthors:Seonghun Jung, Sieun Moon, Jiyoung Jeong, Jimin Lee, Jaehyuk Huh\n\nFigures and Tables:\n\n[Input: Ask the author(s) a question! :)]\n\nAsk\n\nAbstract:Vision-language-action (VLA) models translate multimodal observations into low-level robot actions. During robot operation, each control period sets an inference deadline, and overruns leave the robot acting on stale observations, reducing task success. Meeting this deadline motivates on-device or nearby edge execution, where a single robot requires batch-1 inference outside the design point of LLM serving systems. Although VLA architectures combine familiar vision-language, autoregressive, and diffusion-style components, their runtime behavior in this batch-1 control setting remains uncharacterized. We characterize four representative VLA models on an edge GPU server and two onboard SoCs, using single-inference profiling and 43,200 closed-loop episodes. Action tensor dimensionality determines whether a stage is memory- or compute-bound, platform balance can shift that bottleneck, and GPU frequency scaling yields a platform-dependent energy-latency sweet spot. In closed-loop operation, overlapping inference with action execution creates an accuracy-speed-energy tradeoff, and no configuration is Pareto-dominant across deployment SLOs. These results guide joint design of VLA model architectures, hardware, and runtime policies.\n\n* To appear in the Proceedings of the 32nd ACM International Conference on Architectural Support for Programming Languages and Operating Systems (ASPLOS '27). 17 pages, 14 figures  \n\nFull-text Links:View paper on--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCharacterizing Vision-Language-Action Models across XPUs: Constraints and Acceleration for On-Robot Deployment (https://shoepaly.app/?_=%2Fpdf%2F2604.24447%23hKr38k1yZITo%2FkpxGBdjJos%3D)\nciteturn32107search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Characterizing Vision-Language-Action Models across XPUs: ... sis for low-cost VLA deployment via model–hardwareco-characterization. ... arXiv:2604.24447v1  [cs.RO]  27 Apr 2026 ... (VLA) models (Sapkota et al., 2025).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNExT-Game 2026: New Frontiers in Game-Theoretic Learning – ICML 2026 Workshop 1–8 (https://openreview.net/pdf/674934bf5433cf3354be6b7f13ce52737b2d6b0c.pdf)\nciteturn32107search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; How controllable arelarge language models?a unified evaluation across behavioral granularities, 2026.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBenchmarking Affordance Generalization with (https://thrlvemurket.com/?_=%2Fpdf%2F2602.05441%232LHwtQTrZ4XkLaecG94sHNc%3D)\nciteturn32107search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; courage the research community to evaluate their own VLAs on BusyBox and to ... arXiv:2602.05441v1  [cs.RO]  5 Feb 2026 ... from language and vision, e.g., characterizing a VLA’s ability to comprehend new instructions that\n"
